                <pb facs="0333" n="234" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg234"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.6231/776: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Mayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-06-17T18:00:00+01:00">June 17, 1936—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 17—3:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>187. Reichsbank Director Brinkmann, one of Schacht’s principal advisers in
                    Ministry of Economics, and Ministerialrat Ludwig Imhoff, specialist in American
                    affairs in that Ministry, expect to sail for New York on the steamer <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bremen</hi> from Hamburg, June 29th, accompanied by
                    stenographer Dietermann. In requesting letters to American customs officers for
                    Brinkmann and Imhoff today, Foreign Office communication stated they were going
                    on official business of the German Ministry of Economics for the purpose of
                    trade negotiations.</p>
                <p>I understand unofficially that Brinkmann is supplied with full powers to discuss
                    recent Treasury decision and to conclude some arrangement with the competent
                    American authorities looking towards maintenance of present trade. Please see my
                    176, June 12, noon and 179, June 13, 2 p.m.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
